<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Lullaby · c. 1780 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Frère Jacques</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A French round about a sleepy friar who must wake up and ring the morning bells. It is sung by children all over the world.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>anonymous (possibly Jean-Philippe Rameau)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>French children's song, sung worldwide</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>France</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>melody c. 1780 (manuscript of 1775–1785); words printed 1825, words and music together 1869 (Lebouc's book 1860)</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Lullaby</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (lyrics traditional (words printed by 1825))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frere-jacques">Frère Jacques</a>&quot;, known in English as &quot;Brother John&quot;, is a nursery rhyme of French origin, traditionally sung as a round. It is about a friar who has overslept and is urged to wake and ring the bell for matins, the night or early-morning prayers. The common English version keeps the rhythm but changes the meaning, so that the bells wake Brother John. The French frère, &quot;brother&quot;, is the root of the English word friar.</p><p>The oldest known version of the tune is in a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of vaudeville tunes in the Bibliothèque nationale in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, dated to between 1775 and 1785, where it is called &quot;Frère Blaise&quot;. According to the sheet-music collector James Fuld, the tune was first published in 1811; an 1825 publication gave the words with the melody described in solfège, and words and music were printed together in Paris in 1869. They also appear together in Charles Lebouc's popular children's book Récréations de l'enfance, first published in 1860. The musicologist Sylvie Bouissou found a manuscript in the French National Library that lists &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frere-jacques">Frère Jacques</a>&quot; among 86 canons under the name of Jean-Philippe Rameau, some evidence that he wrote the music. The tune resembles Girolamo Frescobaldi's Capriccio Fra Jacopino, published around 1615, as well as a French song of 1650 and a Hungarian folk tune.</p><p>Who the friar was is not known. A link to the 17th-century lithotomist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frere-jacques">Frère Jacques</a> Beaulieu has often been claimed, but a study found no evidence for it. Others have suggested that the song mocked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican friars</a>, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobin-club">Jacobins</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, for idleness and comfort. The satirical Russian pseudonym Kozma Prutkov claimed it came from a Russian seminary song.</p><p>The round is one of the simplest repeating canons, and that simplicity helped it travel. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> its tune is so familiar that it is widely taken for a Chinese folk song, &quot;Two Tigers&quot;, and children in China, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and elsewhere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> sing their own words to it. In 1926 officers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/whampoa-military-academy">Whampoa Military Academy</a> set to it the patriotic song &quot;Revolution of the Citizens&quot;, associated with the First United Front between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a>, and demonstrators in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiananmen">Tiananmen</a> Square chanted political slogans to it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-mahler">Gustav Mahler</a> used it, in a minor key and under its German name &quot;Bruder Martin&quot;, in the third movement of his Symphony No. 1; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonard-bernstein">Leonard Bernstein</a> used it to teach counterpoint on television; and the Beatles sang its title under the last verse of &quot;Paperback Writer&quot; (1966).</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A short, teasing wake-up song: a friar who should be calling his house to early prayers has overslept, and the singers rouse him. Sung as a round, the overlapping voices sound like a peal of bells.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">French</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">French</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Frère Jacques, frère Jacques,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Brother Jacques, Brother Jacques,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Dormez-vous ? dormez-vous ?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Are you sleeping? Are you sleeping?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Sonnez les matines ; sonnez les matines ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ring the matins; ring the matins;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Din din don. Din din don.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ding ding dong. Ding ding dong.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Traditional. Text from <a href="https://fr.wikisource.org/wiki/Fr%C3%A8re_Jacques_(texte)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (3)</summary><ul><li>line 1: 'Frère' is a friar or monk, 'Brother' as a title; Jacques is kept as a name (English versions of the round often call him John).</li><li>line 3: 'Sonnez les matines' is an imperative: ring (the bell for) matins, the night or early-morning prayer office. He has overslept his duty.</li><li>line 4: 'Din din don' is the sound of the bell, rendered with the English 'ding ding dong'.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
